<div class=3D"ed_model04 clearfix">
<div class=3D"ed_flex_box">
<div class=3D"box">
<div class=3D"ed_txt">&nbsp;</div>

<div class=3D"ed_txt" style=3D"text-align: justify;">______</div>

<div class=3D"ed_txt" style=3D"text-align: justify;">National Yang Ming Chi=
ao Tung University (NYCU) has built a private cloud platform that supports =
more than 500 students working simultaneously on chip design and simulation=
, bringing classroom training closer to the demands of the semiconductor in=
dustry.&nbsp;<br>
<br>
Developed with Bigstack and powered by servers equipped with AMD EPYC proce=
ssors, the platform combines electronic design automation (EDA) tools with =
high-performance computing (HPC). It supports 18 integrated circuit (IC) co=
urses, giving students mlhb hands-on experience in design workflows for adv=
anced semiconductor processes.<br>
<br>
The initiative receives support from Taiwan=E2=80=99s Ministry of Education=
 under the Taiwan Chip-based Industrial Innovation Program. It addresses a =
growing challenge for semiconductor education: providing students with indu=
stry-relevant computing environments while protecting confidential design d=
ata and keeping systems manageable.<br>
<br>
<strong>Meeting the Demands of Advanced Chip Design</strong><br>
<br>
As courses incorporate FinFET technology and advanced manufacturing process=
es such as 7-nanometer nodes, students need more than conventional circuit =
design training. They must also learn to work within increasingly complex d=
esign rules, software environments and security requirements.<br>
<br>
Ming-Dou Ker, associate dean of NYCU=E2=80=99s College of Electrical and Co=
mputer Engineering, said the EDA tools and workflows used in teaching incre=
asingly resemble those used in industry. That shift raises both computing d=
emands and the need for tighter control over access to sensitive informatio=
n.<br>
<br>
Process models, design rules and process design kits (PDKs)=E2=80=94the fil=
es and data needed to design chips for a specific manufacturing process=E2=
=80=94contain confidential information. Supporting their use in teaching re=
quires a private cloud with centralized management and isolated environment=
s. Yet computing capacity was only part of the challenge.<br>
<br>
=E2=80=9COur biggest problem was not hardware, but staffing,=E2=80=9D said =
Lin-Hung Lai, who holds a doctorate from NYCU=E2=80=99s Institute of Electr=
onics and planned the system. Graduate students previously had to maintain =
servers alongside their research responsibilities. A single host failure co=
uld disrupt classes for hundreds of students, making simpler administration=
 a priority for the new platform.<br>
&nbsp;</div>
</div>

<div class=3D"box">
<div class=3D"ed_txt" style=3D"text-align: justify;">
<div class=3D"ed_model08 clearfix">
<div class=3D"ed_pic_full"><br>
<br>
<strong>Pooling Resources and Automating Routine Tasks</strong><br>
<br>
Built on Bigstack=E2=80=99s CubeCOS platform, the cloud consolidates previo=
usly separate computing resources into a shared pool. It provides isolated =
environments for different courses, balancing performance needs with access=
 controls for confidential process data. The architecture also helps contai=
n disruptions. When a node encounters a problem, maintenance can focus on t=
he affected environment, reducing the risk of interruptions across the teac=
hing platform.<br>
<br>
To further ease the administrative workload, NYCU used Bigstack=E2=80=99s a=
pplication programming interfaces (APIs) to develop its own Account Managem=
ent System. The system automates account requests, activation, access permi=
ssions and password resets. Lai said connecting these processes through API=
s has replaced substantial manual work with software, allowing the team to =
devote more time to teaching support and technical services.<br>
<br>
NYCU currently operates 10 high-performance servers under unified CubeCOS m=
anagement. The deployment supports concurrent design and simulation work by=
 more than 500 students, with room to expand for more complex IC design cou=
rses and future AI applications.<br>
<br>
<strong>Building Infrastructure for Long-Term Semiconductor Education</stro=
ng><br>
<br>
For Lai, a key outcome has been the close working relationship with a Taiwa=
n-based technology team. =E2=80=9CFrom hardware to software, we can discuss=
 problems directly with engineers, and many issues can be resolved very qui=
ckly,=E2=80=9D he said.<br>
<br>
That collaboration has helped the university and its industry partner addre=
ss practical teaching needs together, building expertise that extends beyon=
d installing a new platform. Ker described the project=E2=80=99s central am=
bition: =E2=80=9CWe are not simply providing an experimental environment; w=
e are building a laboratory.=E2=80=9D<br>
<br>
NYCU plans to continue expanding its computing capabilities for AI and adva=
nced semiconductor design. The goal is to establish lasting infrastructure =
that supports teaching, research and closer connections with industry=E2=80=
=94and offers a model other universities can adapt as they modernize semico=
nductor education.<br>
&nbsp;</div>
</div>
